RapidCCNA is a study game for the Cisco CCNA 200-301 exam, built in Godot 4. It lives
in LearnNetworking, the repo where I keep my own networking roadmap, and it exists
because I wanted somewhere to practice that actually checks whether I understand what
I'm doing. It has 42 lessons covering all 53 subtopics on the exam blueprint, 600
original questions, 16 drills, 14 hands-on labs, and a timed mock exam. All of the
question content is written from scratch in exam style, and none of it comes from
real Cisco exams.

The project went through nine milestones, M0 through M8, and the rule running through
all of them is that learning comes before gameplay. Anything that looks like a game
mechanic is there because it makes the studying stick.

## How a session works

Every unit follows the same loop. You get a short tutorial, then a guided drill with
hints, place values and a worked solution after every item, then unguided rounds once
you've had ten attempts with the training wheels on. After that, spaced repetition
(SM-2) brings questions back right before you'd forget them. A unit counts as
mastered at 85% accuracy over your last twenty unguided answers. Units that are pure
theory, with no drill to play, end in a short unit quiz on their own questions
instead, so the daily plan never stalls on them.

The dashboard builds each day's session from what's due, then the next unmastered
unit, always walking the exam areas in blueprint order. When you'd rather go practice
OSPF, the Topics screen opens up every unit, drill and lab on demand, and it feeds the
same progress and review schedule. Under every question there's a Study button that
opens the question's full lesson over the screen and puts you back exactly where you
were. Acronyms in lessons and questions are highlighted and show their full name on
hover.

## The drills

Most of the sixteen drills are generated, so you never run out of items. Each one is
an entry in `drills.json` plus a generator script with a `make_item(rng)` function, and
they all share one screen, so adding a drill needs no UI code. The early ones cover
binary, subnetting, routing tables, wildcard masks, ACLs and NAT. Later ones handle
IPv6, cabling and interface faults, Wi-Fi channel planning, HSRP elections, service
ports and syslog, and JSON and REST.

A few of them are strict on purpose. The IPv6 Workshop grades compression against
RFC 5952, so a valid but longer address is marked wrong, with the reason. The JSON
drill asks whether a snippet is valid and why not, and since Godot's own parser lets
some invalid JSON through, I cross-checked 300 validity verdicts and 100 path answers
against Python's strict parser, with zero mismatches.

The switching and routing puzzles (spanning tree, OSPF, EtherChannel and MAC address
tables) sit on small pure-logic engines in `core/net`. The labs run on the same
engines, so a drill and a lab can never disagree about who wins a root bridge
election.

## The IOS simulator

The part I'm proudest of is `core/ios`, a simulation of the CCNA slice of Cisco IOS.
It has the real modes and prompts, abbreviations like `sh ip int br`, `do` from any
mode, `?` help, Tab completion, history, and IOS's own error messages, caret marker
included. It covers interfaces, VLANs and trunks, static routes, ACLs, NAT, port
security, single-area OSPF, EtherChannel, Rapid PVST+, CDP and LLDP, HSRP, DHCP, SSH,
NTP, syslog and SNMP, plus output filters like `sh run | s ospf`. `ping` walks each
device's routing table hop by hop, so it fails if either direction is missing a route.

Labs are graded on how the network behaves, not on whether you typed the commands I
had in mind. A check like `route_to` or `ospf_neighbor` asks the simulated network a
question, so any valid configuration passes. Every lab is tested twice: it has to
start unsolved, and its model solution has to complete every task. Eighteen smaller
hidden labs sit behind command-line questions in the mock exam.

Getting the details right meant sweating some state. HSRP elections advance after
every command, so a higher-priority router that joins late stays standby until it has
`preempt`, just like on real hardware. A relayed DHCP lease only works if the server
can route its reply back to the relay. The OSPF router ID is picked when the process
starts and sticks until `clear ip ospf process`, with the same warning IOS gives.
Driving the labs by hand turned up some good bugs too. Godot 4.3's `range()` quietly
truncates to 32 bits, so the DHCP server handed out addresses it was told to exclude,
and `transport input ssh telnet` was being read as just `ssh`, which let a config
with Telnet wide open pass the SSH-only checks.

## The mock exam

The mock exam comes in a full version (100 questions in 120 minutes) and a quick one
(30 in 36). Questions are dealt to each exam area by the blueprint's weights, using
largest remainders so the total always comes out right, then round-robin across
subtopics so one big subtopic can't crowd out the rest. Questions from your last mock
are held back until the fresh ones run out, and command-line tasks are capped at 5%
because they take longer. There's a countdown, no feedback and no going back.

Scores land on Cisco's 300 to 1000 scale with 825 to pass. Cisco doesn't publish how
it scales, so that mapping is my best approximation, and the README says so. The
report shows a per-area breakdown, the area costing you the most points, and every
miss with its explanation, and those answers feed spaced repetition like any other
review.

## Keeping the content honest

Six hundred questions is a lot to keep straight, so most of the rules live in CI. A
validator checks every lesson and question against its schema and fails the build if
any subtopic has fewer than five questions or any exam area can't fill a full mock. A
second tool, `qa_content.py`, looks for the problems a schema can't see: a "(Choose
two)" that doesn't match the answer key, stems that mention an exhibit that isn't
there, duplicate choices, explanations too short to teach anything, and typed answers
that only accept one spelling of an interface name.

Its best catch was the answer key giving itself away. When one choice was clearly the
longest, it was the right answer 58% of the time, when it should be closer to 25%, so
always picking the wordiest option was a winning strategy. I rewrote the wrong answers
on 228 questions to be as specific as the right ones, which brought it down to 36%,
and CI now fails if it creeps back over 40%. A separate check fails the build if a
lesson uses an acronym the glossary doesn't explain.

## Accessibility

A settings screen covers text size from 85% to 150%, a high contrast mode where every
text color clears 7:1, reduced motion, and extra time on the mock exam for anyone with
an accommodation. Every look meets WCAG AA, which a test checks, and right and wrong
answers are labeled in words rather than by color alone. Everything can be driven
from the keyboard.

I upgraded to Godot 4.7 mainly for screen reader support through AccessKit. Every
control has a name that makes sense out of context, so a screen reader hears "Network
components: Study unit" instead of a dozen buttons that all say "Study," and results,
terminal output and completed lab tasks are announced as they happen. A test opens
every screen, question type and drill and fails on any control that's unnamed,
duplicated or unreachable with Tab, and a script dumps what Orca actually receives on
Linux. That's how I found that Godot 4.7.0 crashed the moment you answered a question
with a screen reader running, which is why the project needs 4.7.2.

## The architecture

- **Engine.** Godot 4.7.2 and GDScript, with autoloads for settings, content,
  spaced repetition, progress, sessions and display scaling.
- **Logic.** Everything in `core/` is pure logic with no UI: the networking engines,
  the IOS simulator, the grader and the mock exam's selection and scoring.
- **Content.** Lessons, questions, drills and labs are all JSON, validated and
  QA-checked in Python.
- **Tests.** 244 GUT tests, an end-to-end smoke test that plays a daily session and a
  quick mock, and a layout test that opens every screen, question, lesson and lab at
  two window sizes and fails if anything runs off the edge.
- **CI.** GitHub Actions runs the tests, the content checks, the acronym check and a
  zero-warnings gate, and fails if the tests leave orphan nodes behind.
- **Web build.** Exported on every push, about 12 MB gzipped, with progress saved to
  IndexedDB. Smaller windows get a smaller canvas instead of shrunken text, so it
  stays readable on a phone held sideways.

There are still gaps. Screen readers don't work in the web build, because Godot's web
export doesn't support them, and I haven't tested on-screen keyboards on a real phone
or screen readers on Windows and macOS. I wrote up how the whole thing came together
in [From Skeleton to Mock Exam](/blog/rapidccna-in-one-evening).
